"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,175\nThe Learning Brain. Series 7: Digital Learning\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,365\nEpisode 7\n\n3\n00:00:07,445 --> 00:00:09,608\nLaptop or longhand?\n\n4\n00:00:12,408 --> 00:00:14,532\nThe pen is mightier than the keyboard.\n\n5\n00:00:15,065 --> 00:00:22,820\nIn 2014, a study with that title made headlines and changed laptop policies in classrooms everywhere.\n\n6\n00:00:23,401 --> 00:00:26,762\nWhat happened next is a good lesson in how science works.\n\n7\n00:00:26,932 --> 00:00:34,316\nPam Mueller and Daniel Oppenheimer found that students who typed notes wrote more words and copied more of the lecture verbatim.\n\n8\n00:00:34,837 --> 00:00:38,857\nStudents who wrote by hand did better on conceptual questions.\n\n9\n00:00:39,269 --> 00:00:42,170\nThe explanation: handwriting is slower,\n\n10\n00:00:42,377 --> 00:00:44,759\nso you have to choose and rephrase.\n\n11\n00:00:44,929 --> 00:00:47,008\nThen came direct replications.\n\n12\n00:00:47,382 --> 00:00:50,189\nIn 2019, Kayla Morehead,\n\n13\n00:00:50,402 --> 00:00:54,191\nJohn Dunlosky and Katherine Rawson repeated the study closely.\n\n14\n00:00:54,701 --> 00:01:00,089\nThey found only small differences favoring handwriting that were not statistically significant.\n\n15\n00:01:00,653 --> 00:01:07,428\nA preregistered replication in 2021 also found no clear difference on the quiz.\n\n16\n00:01:07,598 --> 00:01:14,615\nThen, in 2024, Abraham Flanigan and colleagues pooled twenty four studies of college students.\n\n17\n00:01:15,049 --> 00:01:17,703\nHandwritten notes were linked to higher achievement,\n\n18\n00:01:17,999 --> 00:01:22,404\nwith a small effect size of about a quarter of a standard deviation.\n\n19\n00:01:22,898 --> 00:01:25,001\nTyping produced far more notes.\n\n20\n00:01:25,297 --> 00:01:29,167\nAnd typed notes almost never included the lecture's diagrams.\n\n21\n00:01:29,337 --> 00:01:30,492\nSo what do we make of it?\n\n22\n00:01:30,901 --> 00:01:34,304\nAcross studies, handwriting seems to have a small edge,\n\n23\n00:01:34,324 --> 00:01:36,396\nas students currently take notes.\n\n24\n00:01:36,795 --> 00:01:38,949\nBut the medium matters less than the method.\n\n25\n00:01:39,406 --> 00:01:44,557\nNotes that select, rephrase and connect ideas beat notes that transcribe,\n\n26\n00:01:44,697 --> 00:01:46,054\nwhatever the device.\n\n27\n00:01:46,224 --> 00:01:47,597\nAnd for some students,\n\n28\n00:01:47,819 --> 00:01:50,955\na laptop is not a preference but an accommodation.\n\n29\n00:01:51,331 --> 00:01:53,403\nAny policy needs to protect that.\n\n30\n00:01:53,573 --> 00:01:54,341\nFor teaching.\n\n31\n00:01:54,702 --> 00:01:56,553\nTeach students how to take notes: main ideas,\n\n32\n00:01:56,759 --> 00:01:58,332\nin their own words,\n\n33\n00:01:58,352 --> 00:02:00,967\nwith sketches of key diagrams.\n\n34\n00:02:01,285 --> 00:02:04,180\nLeave pauses so they can catch up and revise.\n\n35\n00:02:04,504 --> 00:02:08,184\nLet students choose their medium,\n\n36\n00:02:08,526 --> 00:02:10,451\nwhile explaining the trade offs.\n\n37\n00:02:10,669 --> 00:02:12,646\nOne tip to share with students: take fewer notes,\n\n38\n00:02:12,661 --> 00:02:12,970\nin your own words.\n\n39\n00:02:13,140 --> 00:02:15,268\nIt's not the pen or the keyboard.\n\n40\n00:02:15,546 --> 00:02:17,437\nIt's what you do with the words.\n\n"